<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander I of Epirus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 370–<a href="/years/331-bce">331 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Alexander the Molossian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, called the Molossian, was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> in Epirus and the brother-in-law and uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Invited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> to fight the Italic peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he campaigned against the Lucanians, Bruttii and Samnites from 334 BCE. He was killed near Pandosia in 331 BCE, a model for his relative Pyrrhus's later expedition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus.jpg" alt="Alexander I of Epirus" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_the_Molossian_King_of_Epeiros_350_330_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To reconcile with Olympias he married their daughter Cleopatra to her brother <b>Alexander I of Epirus</b>, and at the wedding feast in the theatre of Aigai in 336 BCE he was assassinated by his bodyguard Pausanias of Orestis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought <b>Alexander of Epirus</b> and joined Pyrrhus against Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/bruttii">Bruttii</a></li><li><blockquote>They defeated Thurii near Laus in 389 BCE and killed <b>Alexander of Epirus</b> at Pandosia in 331 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lucanians">Lucanians</a></li><li><blockquote>Taras led the Italiote League and hired Greek commanders, including Archidamus III of Sparta and <b>Alexander of Epirus</b>, against Italic peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/taras">Taras</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Pandosia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.74,20.7&amp;year=-345" aria-label="Epirus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 741 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander I of Epirus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5511 1011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5511 1011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.74,20.7&amp;year=-345">What was here in 345 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
